{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TjnxqQmqhaC39oQ63ZTJEA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose tasks for release 05.4 (checksum quartet: md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum).\n\nLooking at the constraints:\n- Max 1 task\n- Release scope: only 05.4\n- Target 150-250 lines production code, 5-7 files\n- Max 4 PRD sub-requirements per task\n\nI need to find the PRD for the checksum utilities. The release 05.4 has use cases for md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum. These are very similar utilities - they all compute checksums and verify them.\n\n\nSince I don't have the actual PRD content, I'm working from what I know about these coreutils: they follow the same pattern for computing hashes, verifying checksums, and supporting both BSD and GNU output formats. Given that all four utilities are nearly identical except for their hash algorithms, I could structure them around a shared implementation. With only one task allowed, I should prioritize building the foundational library first before the individual utilities, following the principle of libraries before consumers. I'm working through the naming conventions to figure out which PRD IDs correspond to the checksum utilities, but without being able to read the files directly, I'll need to reference them generically while anchoring to the established pattern. Since all four utilities share the same implementation logic except for the hash function itself, starting with md5sum makes sense as it's the first use case and will establish the template for the others. structure and that these are a \"quartet,\" there might be a single PRD like prd012-checksums or similar, or separate ones.\n\nI'll reference required_reading to point the stitch agent to the correct PRDs, and structure the task around implementing md5sum with its differential tests. Actually, per P2: \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" But with only 1 task allowed, I should do the implementation task first.\n\nWait, actually re-reading: the use cases are all spec_complete. I need to propose implementation code tasks. With max 1 task and 4 very similar utilities, the most efficient approach is to implement all four in one task since they differ only in hash algorithm and binary name. But I need to check line counts - each utility would be ~30-40 lines of main.go, so 4 utilities = ~120-160 lines production code, which fits in the 150-250 range.\n\nI'll structure this as implementing all four checksum utilities together since they're trivially similar—same code structure, just different hash imports. Now I need to figure out the PRD IDs. The analysis section lists releases and use cases but not the actual file names. Based on the naming convention prd[NNN]-[feature-name].yaml and existing examples like prd001-testutils, prd008-ls, prd009-du, the checksum utilities probably have a single combined PRD or individual ones. I'll use required_reading to reference the glob pattern or possible names so the stitch agent can locate them.\n\nLet me write out the task proposal now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27604,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27604},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bd6d10-abfc-4d3a-97cb-7de161a9b36d","uuid":"88c5f370-4a52-4a92-a7ae-b165e9835128"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TjnxqQmqhaC39oQ63ZTJEA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that regenerates Unix utilities in Go from formal specs derived from C source, verified via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds utility implementations, `pkg/` provides shared libraries (sys, format, testutils), `magefiles/` drives orchestration, `docs/` holds specs.\n\n**Current state:** Releases 00.0 through 05.3 have specs written/implemented (code status varies). Release 05.4 (checksum quartet: md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum) has `spec_complete` status with no code started.\n\n**Current release:** rel05.4 — all four use cases (md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum) need implementation.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 05.4 is the target. All four checksum utilities share identical structure — they differ only in the hash algorithm (md5, sha1, sha256, sha512) and binary name. The implementation pattern is: read input files or stdin, compute hash, print `<hash>  <filename>` (GNU format), support `--check` mode to verify. These can be implemented together within the line budget since each utility is ~40-50 lines.\n\nWith the 1-task limit, the most impactful first task is implementing all four utilities' production code (no tests — per P2, tests are a separate task).\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement checksum quartet cmd/ binaries (prd md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (all PRDs matching *md5sum*, *sha1sum*, *sha256sum*, *sha512sum*, or *checksum*)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.4-uc001-md5sum.yaml\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.4-uc002-sha1sum.yaml\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.4-uc003-sha256sum.yaml\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.4-uc004-sha512sum.yaml\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-05.4.yaml\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section for differential testing and SIGPIPE patterns)\n      - cmd/basename/main.go (reference for simple utility structure and SIGPIPE pattern)\n      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/md5sum/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: md5sum utility using crypto/md5\n      - path: cmd/sha1sum/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: sha1sum utility using crypto/sha1\n      - path: cmd/sha256sum/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: sha256sum utility using crypto/sha256\n      - path: cmd/sha512sum/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: sha512sum utility using crypto/sha512\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement hash computation mode for each utility. When invoked with file arguments,\n          read each file and print \"<hex-hash>  <filename>\" (two spaces, GNU format) to stdout.\n          When invoked with no file arguments or with \"-\", read from stdin and print\n          \"<hex-hash>  -\". Use the corresponding crypto package (crypto/md5, crypto/sha1,\n          crypto/sha256, crypto/sha512). Match the PRD requirements for hash computation.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement --check (-c) mode per PRD. Read a checksum file (one \"<hash>  <filename>\"\n          per line), recompute the hash for each listed file, and print \"<filename>: OK\" or\n          \"<filename>: FAILED\" to stdout. Print a summary line to stderr if any checks fail.\n          Exit 1 if any file fails verification. Support both GNU two-space format and BSD\n          tag format on input when parsing check files, per PRD.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement --binary (-b) and --text (-t) flags per PRD. On Unix these are\n          no-ops (binary and text mode are identical) but the flag must be accepted\n          without error and the output prefix character must change accordingly\n          (\" \" for text, \"*\" for binary before the filename) to match GNU behavior.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement auxiliary flags per PRD: --quiet (suppress OK lines in check mode),\n          --status (no output in check mode, exit code only), --warn (-w, warn about\n          improperly formatted checksum lines), --strict (exit non-zero for improperly\n          formatted lines), --ignore-missing (ignore missing files in check mode).\n          Support --version and --help. Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the\n          start of main().\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          All four utilities share the same algorithmic structure. Each main.go is\n          self-contained -- do NOT create a shared internal package. The code duplication\n          is minimal (only the hash.New call differs) and keeps each utility independently\n          readable. This matches the project pattern where each cmd/ package is standalone.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Use Go standard library only: crypto/md5, crypto/sha1, crypto/sha256,\n          crypto/sha512, and io.Copy for streaming hash computation. Do not add\n          any external dependencies. Use pkg/sys for SIGPIPE handling only.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          Flag parsing: use the flag package (standard library). Define flags matching\n          GNU coreutils long-flag names. Accept both single-dash and double-dash forms\n          where the PRD specifies short flags.\n      - id: D4\n        text: |\n          Exit codes: 0 on success, 1 on check failure or file-not-found errors.\n          Match GNU coreutils exit code behavior per PRD.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/md5sum/main.go, cmd/sha1sum/main.go, cmd/sha256sum/main.go, cmd/sha512sum/main.go all compile with \"go build ./cmd/...\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: Each utility computes the correct hash when given a file argument and prints GNU-format output \"<hex-hash>  <filename>\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: Each utility reads stdin when no file arguments are provided and prints \"<hex-hash>  -\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: The --check flag reads a checksum file and verifies each entry, printing OK/FAILED per file\n      - id: AC5\n        text: All auxiliary flags (--quiet, --status, --warn, --strict, --ignore-missing, --binary, --text) are accepted without error\n      - id: AC6\n        text: Each utility calls pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main()\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/md5sum/ ./cmd/sha1sum/ ./cmd/sha256sum/ ./cmd/sha512sum/\" reports no issues\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27604,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27604},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bd6d10-abfc-4d3a-97cb-7de161a9b36d","uuid":"d1eccdd0-d230-4fb2-9707-6a96cee9b69f"}
